Subject: Initialize the forward-iterator string representation

Local GCC 13.2 compatibility change for libc++ 10.0.1; not an upstream backport.
Optimized regex_traits construction triggers maybe-uninitialized when max_size()
is called on a partially constructed string. Initialize the representation as
the input-iterator overload already does. __zero() is noexcept and clears only
representation words, not the allocator, which constructors initialize first.
This is not evidence that max_size() reads an uninitialized string length.

Tests cover empty/short/long ranges, stateful allocators and allocation failure
at O2/O3 with warnings as errors. This adds initialization work; performance in
downstream workloads still requires qualification. Reassess on libc++ upgrades.

--- a/string
+++ b/string
@@ -2048,6 +2048,7 @@
 >::type
 basic_string<_CharT, _Traits, _Allocator>::__init(_ForwardIterator __first, _ForwardIterator __last)
 {
+    __zero();
     size_type __sz = static_cast<size_type>(_VSTD::distance(__first, __last));
     if (__sz > max_size())
         this->__throw_length_error();
